# Preserve the canonical source icon without replacing or regenerating it

`Simpsons.ico` is authoritative source evidence. SHAR must not replace it with a
new artistic asset and must not keep a separate icon-generation subsystem merely
to restyle the game. Icon work is relevant only to from-scratch packaging where
a platform requires a derived representation of that same source icon.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Require exact root `Simpsons.ico` as canonical source evidence.
- [x] Treat `uninst.ico` as optional source evidence rather than a normal-player
  requirement.
- [x] Do not replace, redraw, synthesize, or artistically transform the game
  icon.
- [x] Retire unnecessary icon-generation code where safe. If executable format
  conversion remains necessary, move the reusable logic under `src/user/code/`;
  if the operation can be declarative, make the relevant `build.txt` procedure
  authoritative instead. Never duplicate the same icon logic in both places.
- [x] Do not duplicate icon logic between repository build tooling and the
  lightweight user release.
- [x] Keep README from-scratch icon notes technical and terse; normal players do
  not need icon-build instructions.
- [x] Update README if the final build-only icon procedure changes.

## Dependencies

Depends on source preflight and final platform packaging requirements only.

## Evidence

This record supersedes the earlier direction that expected a repository-owned
icon producer to generate replacement platform icon assets. The product
requirement is now preservation, not replacement.

The game manifest owns exact source identity: root `Simpsons.ico` is mandatory
with minimum one and root `uninst.ico` is explicitly optional with minimum zero.
Existing manifest unit/integration coverage proves the exact path/casing rule and
accepts lawful installations without `uninst.ico`.

The obsolete `src/unreal/icon/` function was fully disconnected from repository
build consumers and has been removed. It reconstructed authored SVG replacement
art and emitted platform-specific styled assets, so retaining or moving it would
contradict the preservation contract. A repository architecture test now rejects
reintroduction of that replacement pipeline.

No executable icon-format converter is retained today because final packaging has
not established one as necessary. If a target later proves it needs a different
container, that work must be a deterministic build-only representation of the
admitted `Simpsons.ico`, with one implementation owned by the final user/build
contract rather than a second artwork source. README keeps this boundary terse
and does not expose icon-building steps to normal players.
